WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.416
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:14.500
Welcome to the first Data
Science Hangout of 2023.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:16.098
I hope you're all
having a great week.

00:00:16.098 --> 00:00:17.890
But if we haven't had
a chance to meet yet,

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:19.010
thanks for joining us.

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:19.720
I'm Rachael.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:21.470
It's nice to meet you.

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:24.310
This is an open space for the
whole data science community

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:28.540
to connect and chat about data
science leadership, questions

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:30.940
you're facing, and getting
to learn about what's

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:34.330
going on in the world of data
across different industries.

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:37.870
And so each week, we feature a
different data science leader

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:40.780
as my co-host to help
lead the discussion

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:44.090
and answer any
questions from you all.

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:46.150
So together, we're all
dedicated to making

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:48.970
this a welcoming
environment for everybody,

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:52.150
so I love when we can hear from
everyone no matter your level

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:55.160
of experience or area of work.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:58.220
And if you've been here before,
you've heard this spiel a lot.

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:02.270
But there's always three ways
you can ask questions but also

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:04.080
provide your perspective.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.750
So you can jump in by raising
your hand here on Zoom.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:11.750
You can put questions
into the Zoom chat.

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:14.470
And feel free to just put
a little star next to it

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:17.150
if you wanted me to read it
from the Zoom chat instead.

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:20.650
Otherwise, I could call on you
to introduce yourself and add

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:22.210
some context.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.790
But we also have a Slido link
where you can ask questions

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:26.200
anonymously.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:28.360
And so I see Tyler and Hannah.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:29.110
Thank you.

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:31.950
Both just shared
that in the chat.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:33.950
But we share the
recordings of each session

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:36.260
up to the Posit YouTube,
so you can always

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:39.410
go back and find
helpful resources

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:42.780
and see an overview
of each week.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.380
And we also have a LinkedIn
group for the Hangout,

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:50.860
too, which I'll share in the
chat here in just a second.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:53.860
One other note I wanted to
add here in the beginning

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:56.380
too is, if anybody is
hiring right now and wants

00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:00.040
to share that in the chat, feel
free to put the link to the job

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:02.140
descriptions there.

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:03.850
I was going to
share a link in a--

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:06.190
little bit later
for Chase Carpenter,

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:08.680
who was one of our featured
leaders from the Chicago Cubs.

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:14.040
They're hiring an assistant
director of database marketing.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:17.030
But for our first
Hangout of 2023,

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:21.560
I am very excited to have
Justin Elszasz here joining us.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:26.010
Justin is Chief Data Officer
for the city of Baltimore.

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:28.710
And Justin, I'd love to
have you introduce yourself

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:31.290
and probably fix
where I messed up

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.900
on pronouncing your last
name, and share a little bit

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:37.230
about your role,
and maybe something

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:40.660
you like to do in
your free time, too.

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:41.290
Sure, cool.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:43.000
Thanks for the invite, Rachael.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.340
Yeah, I know we originally
planned on November,

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:48.430
so I didn't intend--

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:52.190
or I don't know if I deserve to
be the first of the new year,

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:54.640
but here we are.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:56.352
Yeah, so my name
is Justin Elszasz.

00:02:56.352 --> 00:02:57.310
You pronounced it fine.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:01.030
Just pretend the
Zs aren't there.

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:03.700
I've been-- so my role
is Chief Data Officer

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:04.900
for the City of Baltimore.

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:09.230
I've been with the City for
a little over five years now.

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:12.400
I started off as
the data scientist

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.750
for our innovation team.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:19.330
So about five years ago,
we were given a grant

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:21.070
from Bloomberg
Philanthropies to create

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:22.990
one of their innovation teams.

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:26.620
I think there's about 25 or 30
cities with these innovation

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:29.840
team grants now.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:34.970
So from there, I spent about,
I want to say, two years

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:36.590
in that role.

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:40.250
We then merged our
innovation team with our--

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:43.820
with CitiStat, our
performance management team

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:46.730
and added a third program,
our Data Fellows program,

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:48.440
and I became the deputy
director for what

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:53.995
we call the Office of
Performance and Innovation.

00:03:53.995 --> 00:03:55.870
I spent about a year
and a half in that role,

00:03:55.870 --> 00:04:01.750
and up until then, our
chief data officer position

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:03.880
had been in our IT agency.

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:07.420
So Baltimore City
Information Technology

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:09.860
reporting to our CIO.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:14.590
About two years ago when we
got a new administration--

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:17.290
Mayor Brandon Scott
came in, we also

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:19.930
added a new position called
the City Administrator, which

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:23.830
is kind of like a chief
operating officer for the city.

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:27.490
The decision was made to move
the chief data officer role out

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:32.440
of our IT agency and to report
to our city administrator.

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:35.200
So it definitely kind of
raised the profile of data

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:39.400
within the administration,
provided executive leadership

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:43.610
support around data and how
to use it more strategically.

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:46.525
And so when the role was moved
over to the mayor's office,

00:04:46.525 --> 00:04:47.950
that's when I took the role.

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:51.430
So yeah, coming up on-- coming
up on two years in that role.

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:55.110
That's just incredible.

00:04:55.110 --> 00:05:00.638
So over the last two years,
part of the rationale

00:05:00.638 --> 00:05:02.180
for moving it into
the mayor's office

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:07.370
was to put a little bit more
emphasis obviously on strategy

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:11.240
and how to use data and
analytics quite a bit

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:16.970
more strategically than we had
been, but also data governance.

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:23.430
So we recognized data quality
continues to be an issue.

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:26.060
There are constantly questions--
and this is true of any city

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:28.310
government or any
jurisdiction, really--

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:32.990
around when you can
and can't share data,

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:36.560
what are the best uses, what
uses should we not pursue

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:38.700
of data?

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:43.340
So data governance is also kind
of one of those large buckets

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:46.860
that I'm responsible for.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:50.480
So that's a little bit
about me and the role

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:52.230
here in city government.

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:56.510
We just a few
months ago announced

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:58.470
we'd be using ARPA funding.

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:01.370
So ARPA is American
Rescue Plan Act.

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:04.025
This is federal funding
in response to COVID.

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:11.200
Baltimore received about
$640 million in ARPA funding,

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:13.120
and we're using 2
million of that to create

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:15.050
a digital services team.

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:16.690
So that will be in my office.

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:18.580
We've been recruiting.

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:20.623
Our director of
digital services,

00:06:20.623 --> 00:06:22.540
the city's first director
of digital services,

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:24.640
just started on Monday.

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:25.870
Their name is Shelby Switzer.

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:30.350
They came from the Beck Center
and from US Digital Services.

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:33.680
So really excited to
have them on board.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:35.000
I posted a link in the chat.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:38.200
We are still hiring
for a couple of roles--

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:43.856
lead developer and lead
UX designer in particular.

00:06:43.856 --> 00:06:45.580
So if you or--

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:50.320
if you're interested or know
folks, please reach out, apply.

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:52.870
And I can also talk a
little bit about maybe

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:57.190
some anticipated new
roles later this year

00:06:57.190 --> 00:06:58.810
that would be in my office.

00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:00.490
Fingers crossed.

00:07:00.490 --> 00:07:02.870
But we can get to
that as it comes up.

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:06.280
So yeah, that's kind
of a quick intro.

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:08.410
Happy to take questions, or--

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:10.210
yeah, however however
you all want to roll.

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:12.290
Yeah, awesome.

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:14.770
And what about something you
like to do in your free time,

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:15.898
too?

00:07:15.898 --> 00:07:16.690
Ha ha, free time.

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:20.140
I've got two--
I've got two kids.

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:23.170
One is going to be
five on Saturday,

00:07:23.170 --> 00:07:26.350
and the other one's two
and 1/2, and he's just

00:07:26.350 --> 00:07:30.220
leaning really hard into
that terrible two mode.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:33.010
Just really growing into it.

00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:36.370
So it's-- pandemic has
been fun for raising kids.

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:37.755
A lot of fun.

00:07:37.755 --> 00:07:39.880
It's been a lot [INAUDIBLE].

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:41.860
So that's mostly my free time.

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:46.420
I read a, lot and I'm into
road cycling quite a bit.

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:50.680
But it's mostly the
kids these days.

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:51.820
That's great.

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:54.827
Happy birthday to them.

00:07:54.827 --> 00:07:56.410
While we're waiting
for some questions

00:07:56.410 --> 00:07:58.060
to come in from
everybody, it sounds

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:00.910
like there's a lot of change
across the organization

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:03.250
and a lot of new data roles.

00:08:03.250 --> 00:08:05.620
And I was curious
how you're going

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:10.180
about building data literacy
through city government?

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:12.410
Yeah, that's a great question.

00:08:12.410 --> 00:08:13.900
It has grown by
leaps and bounds,

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:17.620
even just in the five
years since I've been here.

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:20.500
There were a few
things that I've been

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:24.780
kind of either pursuing or--

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:27.115
but there's-- and
then more generally,

00:08:27.115 --> 00:08:28.750
how it's kind of
gone for the city.

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:33.303
So similar to this
group, Rachael,

00:08:33.303 --> 00:08:34.720
one of the first
things I did when

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:38.510
I came into city government was
kind of stealing from my old--

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:41.530
my previous job was--

00:08:41.530 --> 00:08:44.470
so I came into the mayor's
office as a data scientist,

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:47.230
but I didn't know who
were all the data people

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:49.840
and all the different agencies.

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:53.080
So created the-- called it the
Data Center for Excellence,

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:56.305
which was basically
just a once a month--

00:08:56.305 --> 00:08:57.790
basically a Hangout.

00:08:57.790 --> 00:08:59.980
Someone might present
some of the work

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:03.140
that they're involved
in, or maybe a new tool

00:09:03.140 --> 00:09:06.670
they're trying out,
issues they're having.

00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:09.650
So that was one of the first
communities of practice

00:09:09.650 --> 00:09:12.730
we kind of established around
data and city government,

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:15.100
and that was meant
to, again, kind

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:19.600
of connect the data people.

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:21.080
There are a couple of things--

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:22.342
so that was a few years ago.

00:09:22.342 --> 00:09:23.800
There have been a
few other-- like,

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:26.770
we have a Women
in Data group now

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:30.160
forming a community of practice
and a safe space for women

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:32.455
and minorities in
the data-- in data.

00:09:35.410 --> 00:09:39.380
Most recently though, it's been
more of a concerted effort,

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:43.210
especially as I've taken on
the role of Chief Data Officer.

00:09:43.210 --> 00:09:44.660
There are a couple of places.

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:47.770
One is launching a data academy.

00:09:47.770 --> 00:09:51.790
So we have been piloting it
maybe for the last two months

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:54.310
with the Center for
Government Excellence.

00:09:54.310 --> 00:09:56.650
This is another
Bloomberg-funded group,

00:09:56.650 --> 00:09:59.770
but they're at Johns
Hopkins, literally right

00:09:59.770 --> 00:10:02.230
around the corner from me.

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:07.432
And yeah, so we've been
developing a curriculum for--

00:10:07.432 --> 00:10:09.130
for the Data Academy.

00:10:09.130 --> 00:10:11.650
Where we've chosen to focus is--

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:13.780
it'd be very easy
to start creating

00:10:13.780 --> 00:10:16.630
some kind of curriculum that's
for data analysts and data

00:10:16.630 --> 00:10:17.740
scientists, right?

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:21.890
Like, here are the tools
we use in city government,

00:10:21.890 --> 00:10:25.840
here's intermediate
to advanced Power BI,

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:29.180
or whatever the case might be.

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:30.970
But instead, what
we chose to focus

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:33.920
was on folks with little
or no data literacy.

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:38.860
So think about your frontline
workers, your solid waste

00:10:38.860 --> 00:10:43.340
collection crews, your housing
inspector, those folks.

00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:46.332
And so we've named a
few different tracks

00:10:46.332 --> 00:10:48.040
within the academy
that we're working on,

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:50.373
but the first one that we've
really given a lot of focus

00:10:50.373 --> 00:10:53.980
is this kind of
foundations of data--

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:58.300
data literacy, which is really
just about what our data-- how

00:10:58.300 --> 00:10:59.630
does it show up in your life?

00:10:59.630 --> 00:11:02.150
How does it show
up in your work?

00:11:02.150 --> 00:11:07.180
So trying to start at the
very foundational level

00:11:07.180 --> 00:11:10.540
so we can make sure we're
bringing everyone along

00:11:10.540 --> 00:11:14.320
in growing data literacy.

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:17.350
Another area that's-- and
so we're actually right

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:20.320
in the middle of trying to
consolidate a lot of these

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:23.170
disparate efforts around
data literacy into a more

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:26.210
comprehensive
workforce strategy.

00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:29.870
Another component is around
our job classifications.

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:34.510
So in government there's always
your job your functional job

00:11:34.510 --> 00:11:37.600
title might be data scientist,
but for us, we still

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:41.710
have actual classifications
that are more like operations

00:11:41.710 --> 00:11:45.280
manager or operations officer.

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:49.160
We are working with
our IT department

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:53.230
to modernize a lot of tech and
data related classifications

00:11:53.230 --> 00:11:56.560
so that we can then
work with our agencies

00:11:56.560 --> 00:12:01.120
so that they know better what
they're actually looking for.

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:04.876
I know it's similar in
the industry where, if--

00:12:04.876 --> 00:12:07.120
if the organization
is relatively

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:08.620
new to data
scientists, they think

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:11.320
they need a data scientist
first and so they

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:13.090
hire a data scientist.

00:12:13.090 --> 00:12:15.040
But that person might
quickly get burnt out

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:18.070
because what they really
end up doing is entirely

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:21.220
data cleaning or creating
a better data collection

00:12:21.220 --> 00:12:25.133
tool in the first place,
which was kind of my--

00:12:25.133 --> 00:12:26.800
if I'm honest, that
was what my role was

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:29.030
like when I initially came in.

00:12:29.030 --> 00:12:32.020
So getting agencies
to see that there's

00:12:32.020 --> 00:12:38.590
a whole universe of data roles,
and trying to fit the best--

00:12:38.590 --> 00:12:41.090
the best one to their needs.

00:12:41.090 --> 00:12:44.470
So that's another area
where there's a lot about--

00:12:44.470 --> 00:12:47.950
a lot of talk and a lot of
work going into growing data

00:12:47.950 --> 00:12:52.180
literacy within government.

00:12:52.180 --> 00:12:52.940
Thank you.

00:12:52.940 --> 00:12:55.810
I see a lot of chat
going on back and forth,

00:12:55.810 --> 00:12:58.030
and great resources
being shared, too.

00:12:58.030 --> 00:12:58.690
Katherine.

00:12:58.690 --> 00:13:00.800
I know you had a question
earlier in the chat.

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:03.755
Do you want to jump in?

00:13:03.755 --> 00:13:04.930
KATHERINE GERTON: Hey.

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:06.550
I was just bringing up--

00:13:06.550 --> 00:13:08.140
said you were working
with the people

00:13:08.140 --> 00:13:10.990
at Johns Hopkins, that at
the last RStudio conference,

00:13:10.990 --> 00:13:13.480
that organization Data
Trail that presented

00:13:13.480 --> 00:13:15.820
kind of focusing on
many of the things

00:13:15.820 --> 00:13:17.663
that you were speaking to.

00:13:17.663 --> 00:13:19.330
Different job
classifications and things

00:13:19.330 --> 00:13:21.220
like that, not focused
within government,

00:13:21.220 --> 00:13:24.580
but just that it is really kind
of an industry-wide problem

00:13:24.580 --> 00:13:26.800
going from their
focus for interns

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:29.770
all the way up
through any position.

00:13:29.770 --> 00:13:31.270
So I was just sharing
that with you,

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:34.463
and I think somebody else
included the link to the talk

00:13:34.463 --> 00:13:36.130
that there's really
a lot of opportunity

00:13:36.130 --> 00:13:40.007
there for partnership, but
even in your own neighborhood.

00:13:40.007 --> 00:13:42.090
JUSTIN ELSZASZ: Yeah, it's
funny you mention them.

00:13:42.090 --> 00:13:44.180
So I haven't talked
to Jeff in a while.

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:45.530
So Jeff and I actually met--

00:13:48.250 --> 00:13:51.400
gosh, this would have been
maybe three or so years ago.

00:13:51.400 --> 00:13:54.230
I don't think it had been called
the Data Trail at the time,

00:13:54.230 --> 00:13:56.230
but it was a similar--

00:13:56.230 --> 00:13:58.870
I think it might have been
the same or a similar program.

00:13:58.870 --> 00:14:00.160
They were looking to train--

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:02.660
KATHERINE GERTON: Yeah, I think
they went through a rebrand.

00:14:02.660 --> 00:14:03.820
JUSTIN ELSZASZ: Yeah, yeah.

00:14:03.820 --> 00:14:06.940
Training-- yeah, really high
school and college students

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:11.800
from East Baltimore
in data science.

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:15.893
We actually did hire
one on for an intern--

00:14:15.893 --> 00:14:17.560
I think it might have
been an internship

00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:19.810
about three years ago.

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:22.030
Yeah, it's a great idea.

00:14:22.030 --> 00:14:24.820
In fact, I think the
person we had hired

00:14:24.820 --> 00:14:27.670
had started in on college.

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:31.030
They were pursuing-- they had
started pursuing a degree in--

00:14:31.030 --> 00:14:33.100
I want to say it was music or--

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:35.800
music or recording,
sound recording.

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:37.840
Decided that wasn't
for them, wasn't sure

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:40.660
what was next, and found out
about this program and said,

00:14:40.660 --> 00:14:44.300
yeah this is actually something
I'm really interested in.

00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:46.960
And so yeah, they spent
some time with us.

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:53.020
And the really cool thing is he
also created a small consulting

00:14:53.020 --> 00:14:53.840
firm.

00:14:53.840 --> 00:14:55.705
They were called
Problem Forward.

00:14:55.705 --> 00:14:57.670
I think they're still around.

00:14:57.670 --> 00:15:01.670
But for those folks who don't--
who go through that program

00:15:01.670 --> 00:15:04.630
but don't find an
immediate role elsewhere,

00:15:04.630 --> 00:15:07.600
Problem Forward was spun up as
a small consulting firm that

00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:10.240
would hire the people that
they've trained in that

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:11.540
program.

00:15:11.540 --> 00:15:13.540
And it was a consulting
firm, so they would just

00:15:13.540 --> 00:15:16.790
take on small projects
with local businesses.

00:15:16.790 --> 00:15:18.040
So it was a really cool model.

00:15:18.040 --> 00:15:20.207
Yeah, we've definitely
worked with them in the past.

00:15:20.207 --> 00:15:25.300
I haven't recently, but yeah,
it's a great reminder that

00:15:25.300 --> 00:15:26.890
yeah, some of--

00:15:26.890 --> 00:15:29.350
this is obviously a national
and international community

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:30.350
around data science--

00:15:30.350 --> 00:15:30.850
science.

00:15:30.850 --> 00:15:36.220
But there's even
hyper-local networks

00:15:36.220 --> 00:15:37.960
of data science going on, too.

00:15:41.080 --> 00:15:42.920
Thank you.

00:15:42.920 --> 00:15:47.050
I'm seeing a few plus ones to
Alan's question from earlier.

00:15:47.050 --> 00:15:48.580
And Alan, I'd love
to have you jump

00:15:48.580 --> 00:15:51.520
in next with your question.

00:15:51.520 --> 00:15:52.510
Sure.

00:15:52.510 --> 00:15:54.628
Hey Justin, hey everybody.

00:15:54.628 --> 00:15:55.420
I'm really curious.

00:15:55.420 --> 00:15:58.240
Maybe Justin, if you could sort
of blow up super high level

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:00.743
and talk about what's
the mission of the office

00:16:00.743 --> 00:16:02.410
that you're in, and
how does that relate

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:04.675
to who your customers are?

00:16:04.675 --> 00:16:07.090
Are those residents
of the city, are they

00:16:07.090 --> 00:16:10.772
agencies and employees?

00:16:10.772 --> 00:16:13.665
What does the-- what are the
things you're doing look like,

00:16:13.665 --> 00:16:15.040
and how does that--
how does that

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:16.570
roll out to all of those people?

00:16:16.570 --> 00:16:17.530
Just to give context.

00:16:17.530 --> 00:16:19.930
And then Raha's got a
great question digging more

00:16:19.930 --> 00:16:22.550
into detail about
real specific stuff.

00:16:22.550 --> 00:16:24.790
And I think like her,
I've got tons of questions

00:16:24.790 --> 00:16:28.490
about doing data at a city
level, and what that's like.

00:16:28.490 --> 00:16:31.030
So some of that high-level
context would be great.

00:16:31.030 --> 00:16:31.840
Thanks.

00:16:31.840 --> 00:16:35.180
Sure, yeah.

00:16:35.180 --> 00:16:37.640
Yeah, so maybe-- step
back and give you

00:16:37.640 --> 00:16:40.430
a little bit of the
organizational structure.

00:16:40.430 --> 00:16:42.560
So like I said,
I report directly

00:16:42.560 --> 00:16:46.220
to the city administrator who
is appointed by our mayor.

00:16:46.220 --> 00:16:48.830
So I'm in the mayor's cabinet.

00:16:48.830 --> 00:16:51.913
There's also-- but because
the role was just moved over

00:16:51.913 --> 00:16:53.330
less than two years
ago, it didn't

00:16:53.330 --> 00:16:55.110
come with any other roles.

00:16:55.110 --> 00:16:58.820
So until we've now started
hiring for the Digital Services

00:16:58.820 --> 00:17:03.630
team, I've been an office of
one as the Chief Data Officer.

00:17:03.630 --> 00:17:05.609
The office that I came
from, the Mayor's Office

00:17:05.609 --> 00:17:07.650
of Performance and Innovation--

00:17:07.650 --> 00:17:10.650
like I mentioned, they have our
performance management shop,

00:17:10.650 --> 00:17:11.310
CitiStat.

00:17:11.310 --> 00:17:15.599
So they're looking
at broad KPIs more

00:17:15.599 --> 00:17:20.020
as a way of either identifying
issues or holding agencies

00:17:20.020 --> 00:17:23.530
accountable for performance.

00:17:23.530 --> 00:17:26.329
There's-- and then the
Data Fellows program,

00:17:26.329 --> 00:17:29.300
which is another effort we
made a couple of years ago.

00:17:29.300 --> 00:17:33.018
We created that program to
hire in entry level data folks

00:17:33.018 --> 00:17:34.810
to get them in the door
to city government,

00:17:34.810 --> 00:17:39.650
and then deploy them to
agencies that needed help.

00:17:39.650 --> 00:17:42.950
And then our innovation team.

00:17:42.950 --> 00:17:48.440
So-- and then of course,
there's our IT agency, BCIT.

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:52.730
There are-- in any given agency
there are other data analysts

00:17:52.730 --> 00:17:53.630
working with--

00:17:53.630 --> 00:17:56.210
who become kind of subject
matter experts in their areas,

00:17:56.210 --> 00:17:57.440
their domains.

00:17:57.440 --> 00:18:01.460
My role-- it's a
little bit of both.

00:18:01.460 --> 00:18:03.170
And I'm hoping by
the end of the year

00:18:03.170 --> 00:18:06.140
it's going to be almost
kind of like 50/50.

00:18:06.140 --> 00:18:10.250
So right now, me personally
being responsible

00:18:10.250 --> 00:18:12.170
for things like data
governance and capacity

00:18:12.170 --> 00:18:14.660
building-- those are both
things that are a little bit

00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:17.300
inward-focused.

00:18:17.300 --> 00:18:20.690
However, with building the
digital services team--

00:18:20.690 --> 00:18:24.800
that is about building better
customer service and better

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:27.830
digital experiences
for residents.

00:18:27.830 --> 00:18:30.380
So that will have
probably a resident.

00:18:30.380 --> 00:18:33.740
But we're not-- we're not,
like, restricted to that.

00:18:33.740 --> 00:18:36.260
But chances are, we'll
be building things

00:18:36.260 --> 00:18:41.810
that matter, that improve
services directly to residents.

00:18:41.810 --> 00:18:45.260
I mentioned 50/50 because
by the end of the year,

00:18:45.260 --> 00:18:47.330
hopefully in this
new budget year

00:18:47.330 --> 00:18:51.890
we'll be creating a more proper
data science team in my office.

00:18:51.890 --> 00:18:58.220
So like I mentioned,
our CitiStat team really

00:18:58.220 --> 00:19:02.390
wrestles with more high-level
KPIs and performance

00:19:02.390 --> 00:19:06.050
at a higher level.

00:19:06.050 --> 00:19:09.110
I think they've always wanted
to dive more deeply into data,

00:19:09.110 --> 00:19:10.970
but these--

00:19:10.970 --> 00:19:13.880
a lot of the performance stuff
happens on certain cycles.

00:19:13.880 --> 00:19:17.210
So Police Stat is a meeting
that happens every two weeks.

00:19:17.210 --> 00:19:20.810
You can't deliver a deep
insight or create new tools

00:19:20.810 --> 00:19:24.060
on a two-week cycle.

00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:28.880
Same with Clean Stat, which
is about cleaning the city.

00:19:28.880 --> 00:19:32.210
So what I proposed is
creating a small analytic shop

00:19:32.210 --> 00:19:35.180
under my office
that, whereas data--

00:19:35.180 --> 00:19:37.640
our digital services team
would be about improving

00:19:37.640 --> 00:19:42.080
resident-facing
services, and this is--

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:44.630
part of this is just a
way of making the case

00:19:44.630 --> 00:19:47.030
that it's cost-effective.

00:19:47.030 --> 00:19:51.380
But the analytics
shop I'm imagining

00:19:51.380 --> 00:19:57.020
being more about efficiency
and more internally focused.

00:19:57.020 --> 00:20:01.118
So helping agencies find--

00:20:01.118 --> 00:20:02.870
maybe it's detecting
fraud, maybe

00:20:02.870 --> 00:20:08.600
it's optimizing routes for
snowplows, that kind of thing.

00:20:08.600 --> 00:20:12.830
So we have that expertise
in a few different spots,

00:20:12.830 --> 00:20:17.490
but because it's so
sporadic, those folks

00:20:17.490 --> 00:20:20.340
are often pulled into just
day-to-day operations or just

00:20:20.340 --> 00:20:21.600
the regular reporting.

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:24.278
And we still don't--

00:20:24.278 --> 00:20:25.820
even though we would
like to, we just

00:20:25.820 --> 00:20:29.930
don't have the capacity
to set aside and say,

00:20:29.930 --> 00:20:32.810
take this problem and run
for-- run with it for a month

00:20:32.810 --> 00:20:35.450
and come back to us and
see what you've got.

00:20:35.450 --> 00:20:36.950
So that's what I'm
looking to build,

00:20:36.950 --> 00:20:39.620
and hopefully we'll be hiring
for three or four roles

00:20:39.620 --> 00:20:42.000
for that by the end of the year.

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:42.500
Cool.

00:20:42.500 --> 00:20:44.250
JUSTIN ELSZASZ: Does that
answer your question?

00:20:44.250 --> 00:20:45.020
Yeah, it does.

00:20:45.020 --> 00:20:46.770
It gave me a good sense
for where you are.

00:20:46.770 --> 00:20:48.560
And I think that
vision of what does

00:20:48.560 --> 00:20:53.240
a team look like that can do
more and have sort of room

00:20:53.240 --> 00:20:56.810
to run a little bit
sounds really exciting.

00:20:56.810 --> 00:21:00.403
Yeah, I'm excited too
because that's what--

00:21:00.403 --> 00:21:02.820
I mean, that's a little bit
of what I had done previously,

00:21:02.820 --> 00:21:03.590
and so--

00:21:03.590 --> 00:21:08.000
and that's what I always
imagined data analytics

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:09.090
in the city looking like.

00:21:09.090 --> 00:21:11.580
And it definitely
does in other places,

00:21:11.580 --> 00:21:15.880
so it's a little bit of
getting us up to speed.

00:21:15.880 --> 00:21:19.040
Now that said, there are going
to be-- the opportunities

00:21:19.040 --> 00:21:25.490
to do that kind of thing are
much more rare than performance

00:21:25.490 --> 00:21:27.800
management or just
simple data analysis.

00:21:27.800 --> 00:21:34.870
Because again, there's-- it's
finding the right combination

00:21:34.870 --> 00:21:42.310
of high enough data quality and
an agency who their leadership

00:21:42.310 --> 00:21:47.290
and their operations will be
able to adapt and use that kind

00:21:47.290 --> 00:21:48.520
of analysis or tools.

00:21:48.520 --> 00:21:50.980
Because leadership,
it's on a spectrum.

00:21:50.980 --> 00:21:53.560
Not every agency-- not every--

00:21:53.560 --> 00:21:58.410
not all of their
heads are ready to use

00:21:58.410 --> 00:22:02.950
the next generation of tools to
optimize our resources better.

00:22:02.950 --> 00:22:06.010
So yeah, they'll be--

00:22:06.010 --> 00:22:08.970
but that said, there are
those use cases out there,

00:22:08.970 --> 00:22:10.380
and I've got a
couple of examples

00:22:10.380 --> 00:22:12.380
I'm happy to share that
we've worked on already.

00:22:12.380 --> 00:22:16.590
But yeah, that's what I'm
looking forward to this year.

00:22:16.590 --> 00:22:17.680
Cool.

00:22:17.680 --> 00:22:18.180
Thank you.

00:22:18.180 --> 00:22:19.500
JUSTIN ELSZASZ: Yeah.

00:22:19.500 --> 00:22:20.370
Thank you.

00:22:20.370 --> 00:22:24.460
I'm really interested in
that snowplow optimization

00:22:24.460 --> 00:22:26.220
example [INAUDIBLE].

00:22:26.220 --> 00:22:29.970
Other cities-- other
cities, especially places like

00:22:29.970 --> 00:22:30.670
Rochester--

00:22:33.570 --> 00:22:40.125
a lot of Northern cities have
wrestled with that for sure.

00:22:40.125 --> 00:22:42.750
Baltimore is one of those cities
where we get a dusting of snow

00:22:42.750 --> 00:22:45.870
and the whole city shuts
down because it happens

00:22:45.870 --> 00:22:48.210
so infrequently that we don't--

00:22:48.210 --> 00:22:51.240
we can't build up that
amount of capacity

00:22:51.240 --> 00:22:54.300
to deal with it for the rare
times we get lots of snow.

00:22:54.300 --> 00:22:57.330
But places like Rochester
where it's like day in

00:22:57.330 --> 00:22:59.972
and day out for six months,
yeah, they've definitely

00:22:59.972 --> 00:23:00.930
worked on those routes.

00:23:03.615 --> 00:23:05.917
I see a few anonymous
questions that came through,

00:23:05.917 --> 00:23:07.500
and I to make sure
I get to those too.

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:12.000
And one was, what about hardware
and cloud infrastructure?

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:14.430
Do you have to deal
with legacy systems?

00:23:14.430 --> 00:23:17.400
Does the new money
allow you to upgrade?

00:23:17.400 --> 00:23:19.440
Yeah, good question.

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:23.250
Yes, about legacy systems.

00:23:23.250 --> 00:23:26.560
This year, our IT
department-- so there's--

00:23:26.560 --> 00:23:30.450
I have a little bit-- so chief
data officer roles kind of run

00:23:30.450 --> 00:23:32.520
the spectrum in terms
of responsibilities

00:23:32.520 --> 00:23:33.990
and where they
are, where they're

00:23:33.990 --> 00:23:35.820
situated organizationally.

00:23:35.820 --> 00:23:39.960
I have a little bit less
control over architecture.

00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:46.575
That said, yes, there are
tons of legacy applications

00:23:46.575 --> 00:23:48.630
and infrastructure to deal with.

00:23:48.630 --> 00:23:51.810
This year, we're
targeting finally

00:23:51.810 --> 00:23:56.580
decommissioning our mainframe,
which right now still runs

00:23:56.580 --> 00:23:58.770
some of our
environmental citations

00:23:58.770 --> 00:24:00.300
and some of our
revenue collections

00:24:00.300 --> 00:24:02.890
and that kind of thing.

00:24:02.890 --> 00:24:06.300
So we're looking
to replace that.

00:24:06.300 --> 00:24:08.790
There's also a legacy system
with our housing department

00:24:08.790 --> 00:24:13.440
that was home-grown
by one person

00:24:13.440 --> 00:24:16.560
that Housing
Department had hired

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:18.120
who was there for 20 years.

00:24:18.120 --> 00:24:19.620
Wasn't well-documented.

00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:22.500
They of course
left, and now we're

00:24:22.500 --> 00:24:24.390
kind of picking up the
scraps on that system.

00:24:24.390 --> 00:24:27.960
And it does all of
our code enforcement

00:24:27.960 --> 00:24:30.720
and a whole host
of other Housing--

00:24:30.720 --> 00:24:32.410
Housing Department functions.

00:24:32.410 --> 00:24:36.700
So yeah, legacy systems
continue to be a challenge.

00:24:36.700 --> 00:24:41.190
Luckily we have an IT department
that's kind of on it right now

00:24:41.190 --> 00:24:45.940
and has plans to-- to
mitigate a lot of that.

00:24:45.940 --> 00:24:49.500
And so as those
things get replaced,

00:24:49.500 --> 00:24:52.800
we only have up to go in
terms of data quality.

00:24:52.800 --> 00:24:54.480
We just, over the
last two years,

00:24:54.480 --> 00:24:56.970
have rolled out Workday
as our new ERP--

00:24:56.970 --> 00:24:59.670
enterprise resource
planning system.

00:24:59.670 --> 00:25:01.560
That in and of
itself I think has

00:25:01.560 --> 00:25:06.380
addressed a lot of finance
and HR data quality issues.

00:25:06.380 --> 00:25:09.370
So yeah, we're making
moves in that direction.

00:25:09.370 --> 00:25:10.830
Yes, there's legacy systems.

00:25:10.830 --> 00:25:15.690
But I challenge you all
to find a chief data

00:25:15.690 --> 00:25:18.150
officer in another
city who doesn't

00:25:18.150 --> 00:25:20.640
reckon with that sometimes.

00:25:20.640 --> 00:25:24.260
And as far as cloud
goes, yeah, we--

00:25:24.260 --> 00:25:27.610
so for one, our GIS
team is working hard

00:25:27.610 --> 00:25:31.060
on moving out a lot
of our GIS layers

00:25:31.060 --> 00:25:34.030
and infrastructure
over to cloud.

00:25:34.030 --> 00:25:38.740
Of course it's with Esri,
as is our Open Baltimore,

00:25:38.740 --> 00:25:41.430
our open data platform.

00:25:41.430 --> 00:25:44.580
We're-- I think it's --
picking your battles and where

00:25:44.580 --> 00:25:47.080
you're going to get the most
bang for the buck at the moment

00:25:47.080 --> 00:25:48.740
for us.

00:25:48.740 --> 00:25:54.760
I'm looking to pilot a couple
of tools to move to cloud.

00:25:54.760 --> 00:26:00.330
So one that I'll mention is
we, over the summer, partnered

00:26:00.330 --> 00:26:03.000
with a group at Carnegie
Mellon called the Data

00:26:03.000 --> 00:26:04.065
Science for Social Good.

00:26:06.403 --> 00:26:08.070
You may know this
already, but Baltimore

00:26:08.070 --> 00:26:09.870
has a lot of vacant buildings.

00:26:09.870 --> 00:26:13.290
And when a building sits
vacant for long enough,

00:26:13.290 --> 00:26:15.840
rooftops tend to
become an issue.

00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:18.240
And back in January
of last year,

00:26:18.240 --> 00:26:22.250
we had three firefighters
killed when a rooftop was in--

00:26:22.250 --> 00:26:23.940
it was a fully-involved fire.

00:26:23.940 --> 00:26:26.880
Rooftop collapsed on the three
firefighters and were killed.

00:26:26.880 --> 00:26:30.630
So we're using we partnered
with Carnegie Mellon

00:26:30.630 --> 00:26:35.700
to develop an AI tool that will
detect collapsed roofs based

00:26:35.700 --> 00:26:41.060
on our aerial flyover imagery.

00:26:41.060 --> 00:26:43.490
Well, every year we get
new flyover imagery,

00:26:43.490 --> 00:26:49.592
which means every year will
want to refine and update the--

00:26:49.592 --> 00:26:52.710
the model and get
new predictions

00:26:52.710 --> 00:26:55.535
so we can find any new buildings
that may have rooftop issues.

00:26:58.180 --> 00:27:00.520
But we need better--

00:27:00.520 --> 00:27:03.010
like, we're not--
we can't run that

00:27:03.010 --> 00:27:04.600
on someone's local machine.

00:27:04.600 --> 00:27:07.030
So that's a good
opportunity to start

00:27:07.030 --> 00:27:11.860
pushing the bounds of
what we can do in cloud.

00:27:11.860 --> 00:27:13.540
So that's one that I'm--

00:27:13.540 --> 00:27:15.970
we're looking for
a couple of places

00:27:15.970 --> 00:27:19.110
to definitely move that way.

00:27:19.110 --> 00:27:21.390
Thank you for that example.

00:27:21.390 --> 00:27:24.090
And I'm seeing quite
a few questions coming

00:27:24.090 --> 00:27:25.530
into the Zoom chat now, too.

00:27:25.530 --> 00:27:27.690
And I think Mike, you
maybe asked first,

00:27:27.690 --> 00:27:29.685
so I'll hand it
over to you first.

00:27:33.900 --> 00:27:35.920
Hi there.

00:27:35.920 --> 00:27:39.270
So this is, I guess,
a topical question

00:27:39.270 --> 00:27:40.770
for our first
session in January.

00:27:40.770 --> 00:27:42.930
It's goal setting season.

00:27:42.930 --> 00:27:47.700
So how do you balance--

00:27:47.700 --> 00:27:49.950
it's a newish
team, so how do you

00:27:49.950 --> 00:27:55.080
balance goals about
setting up your shop

00:27:55.080 --> 00:27:59.670
and making your infrastructure
great and easy to work with,

00:27:59.670 --> 00:28:05.760
and the goals that are I
guess the kind of higher-up,

00:28:05.760 --> 00:28:08.940
or your customers or your
key stakeholders-- the things

00:28:08.940 --> 00:28:09.600
they want?

00:28:09.600 --> 00:28:12.165
Because I'm right in the
middle of that dilemma,

00:28:12.165 --> 00:28:15.290
and it's tricky, right?

00:28:15.290 --> 00:28:15.790
Yeah.

00:28:15.790 --> 00:28:17.490
Yeah, for sure.

00:28:20.970 --> 00:28:24.000
One is-- I mean, if
everything's a priority,

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:25.110
nothing is a priority.

00:28:25.110 --> 00:28:28.170
But at least having
enough of a mix

00:28:28.170 --> 00:28:30.375
that some things are--
some things are for you,

00:28:30.375 --> 00:28:31.500
some things are for others.

00:28:34.320 --> 00:28:37.710
I'm kind of looking
at some of my own--

00:28:37.710 --> 00:28:40.260
like I said, so up until now,
we've been in office of one.

00:28:40.260 --> 00:28:46.320
So it's-- in some ways, these
are like the office OKRs,

00:28:46.320 --> 00:28:49.110
and in some ways they're like
personal OKRs because I'm just

00:28:49.110 --> 00:28:51.696
one person.

00:28:51.696 --> 00:28:55.410
But yeah, so in terms of
the Digital Services team

00:28:55.410 --> 00:28:59.880
and onboarding and getting
that just stood up,

00:28:59.880 --> 00:29:04.470
we're lucky enough that the
mayor and city administrator

00:29:04.470 --> 00:29:06.540
needed to sign off
on that spending

00:29:06.540 --> 00:29:08.070
because it's ARPA funding.

00:29:08.070 --> 00:29:14.910
And so-- and not only that,
but like all aware of one,

00:29:14.910 --> 00:29:17.310
hiring challenges right
now in the market.

00:29:17.310 --> 00:29:23.550
But two, just hiring process
in terms of city functions.

00:29:23.550 --> 00:29:27.780
And so-- so they
rightly recognize

00:29:27.780 --> 00:29:30.975
that actually getting that
team stood up, hiring people

00:29:30.975 --> 00:29:31.950
is a win.

00:29:31.950 --> 00:29:36.180
And may not have built a
new service for a resident,

00:29:36.180 --> 00:29:39.280
or fixed anything yet,
but that is a win.

00:29:39.280 --> 00:29:44.620
And I think we're lucky enough
that our mayor recognizes that.

00:29:44.620 --> 00:29:46.620
So we have kind of
executive leadership

00:29:46.620 --> 00:29:49.470
that recognizes-- that
is willing to celebrate

00:29:49.470 --> 00:29:53.085
some of the smaller,
more inward-focused wins.

00:29:55.950 --> 00:29:58.830
And then, yeah,
there's a pretty--

00:29:58.830 --> 00:30:02.380
and honestly, for some of them,
there is a pretty direct--

00:30:02.380 --> 00:30:07.320
even though it sounds ostensibly
about internal capacity

00:30:07.320 --> 00:30:08.880
or being inward-focused,
so there's

00:30:08.880 --> 00:30:12.870
a pretty direct line and
theory of change from,

00:30:12.870 --> 00:30:15.750
if we improve data literacy
by building this Data Academy

00:30:15.750 --> 00:30:22.140
program, we will improve service
delivery to our residents.

00:30:22.140 --> 00:30:24.570
Or if we create this
digital services team,

00:30:24.570 --> 00:30:27.660
and even getting a handful of
new experts in technology--

00:30:27.660 --> 00:30:30.240
in civic technology,
and we will build

00:30:30.240 --> 00:30:33.450
better services for residents.

00:30:33.450 --> 00:30:36.180
So now that's-- I'm
lucky enough, again,

00:30:36.180 --> 00:30:41.810
being in the public
sector space where it's--

00:30:44.480 --> 00:30:46.405
if you can't draw
that line, chances

00:30:46.405 --> 00:30:48.530
are you aren't doing that
thing in the first place.

00:30:48.530 --> 00:30:52.110
Or you're not setting that goal
in the first place, you know?

00:30:52.110 --> 00:30:55.940
So I don't know if that helps
or answers the question,

00:30:55.940 --> 00:30:59.630
but those are a couple initial
thoughts on it, I guess.

00:31:03.460 --> 00:31:06.201
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Awesome.

00:31:06.201 --> 00:31:09.050
Raha, I see you had asked a
question in the chat as well.

00:31:09.050 --> 00:31:11.810
Would you want to jump in next?

00:31:11.810 --> 00:31:12.310
Sure.

00:31:12.310 --> 00:31:14.860
Good timing I just ran down
to make sure my Amazon package

00:31:14.860 --> 00:31:15.970
wasn't stolen.

00:31:15.970 --> 00:31:18.610
Oh, I'm glad that it wasn't.

00:31:18.610 --> 00:31:21.160
No, I got there in time.

00:31:21.160 --> 00:31:24.220
No, so I live in Baltimore,
too, and there's definitely

00:31:24.220 --> 00:31:27.040
a lot of issues here that
I'm concerned about that I

00:31:27.040 --> 00:31:29.620
feel data could really address.

00:31:29.620 --> 00:31:32.808
But part of that is
making sure that you're

00:31:32.808 --> 00:31:34.600
able to collect the
right data, that you're

00:31:34.600 --> 00:31:38.320
able to do analysis on it, and
that someone listens to that

00:31:38.320 --> 00:31:40.420
and makes a decision
based off it.

00:31:40.420 --> 00:31:43.780
So I wanted to see if you
had any examples of where

00:31:43.780 --> 00:31:46.600
that has been done, or
where you see it heading.

00:31:46.600 --> 00:31:48.460
Like, will 311 be better?

00:31:48.460 --> 00:31:50.090
Will street sweeping be better?

00:31:50.090 --> 00:31:51.850
Can you make sure
certain neighborhoods

00:31:51.850 --> 00:31:53.885
aren't underserved?

00:31:53.885 --> 00:31:55.135
Yeah, it's a great question.

00:31:59.320 --> 00:32:02.560
Maybe a couple of different
scales I can offer.

00:32:02.560 --> 00:32:05.503
So, like one about--

00:32:05.503 --> 00:32:07.670
well, this would have been
just before the pandemic.

00:32:07.670 --> 00:32:11.230
So I cannot believe
it's been three years.

00:32:11.230 --> 00:32:15.850
We-- so we identified a
pretty big backlog in DPW

00:32:15.850 --> 00:32:20.300
in terms of cleaning service
requests in the city.

00:32:20.300 --> 00:32:21.250
This was upwards of--

00:32:21.250 --> 00:32:25.840
I want to say there were
15 or 17,000 service

00:32:25.840 --> 00:32:28.660
requests that was backlogged.

00:32:28.660 --> 00:32:30.440
So these are things
that had accumulated.

00:32:30.440 --> 00:32:33.430
It was right in the
middle of the summer.

00:32:33.430 --> 00:32:35.340
So a couple of things happened.

00:32:38.560 --> 00:32:43.810
One was we worked
with DPW basically

00:32:43.810 --> 00:32:45.760
to even identify
that as an issue

00:32:45.760 --> 00:32:50.570
and start discussing how we
were going to deal with it.

00:32:50.570 --> 00:32:53.750
And part of that was actually--

00:32:53.750 --> 00:32:55.775
we collected a few
parameters from them.

00:32:55.775 --> 00:32:59.120
And this is more of like
an engineering approach,

00:32:59.120 --> 00:33:00.830
as opposed to a data
science approach.

00:33:00.830 --> 00:33:04.400
But we built a small
heuristic model

00:33:04.400 --> 00:33:06.530
where you could modify
the number of crews that

00:33:06.530 --> 00:33:13.190
were online, and some of
the distribution of where

00:33:13.190 --> 00:33:16.760
the SRs were and that kind
of thing to try and estimate

00:33:16.760 --> 00:33:18.980
how quickly we could
drive things down.

00:33:18.980 --> 00:33:21.770
And so you can manipulate
how many crews you have where

00:33:21.770 --> 00:33:26.930
in the city, and you could see
whether we might or might not

00:33:26.930 --> 00:33:29.510
get to clearing out
the backlog and when.

00:33:29.510 --> 00:33:32.180
Because I think we had
set a particular goal

00:33:32.180 --> 00:33:34.880
of getting that eliminated
by a certain date.

00:33:38.100 --> 00:33:42.120
Yeah, I can't remember
the exact timing.

00:33:42.120 --> 00:33:45.570
So one was-- that
was-- and that was

00:33:45.570 --> 00:33:51.273
a bit of a process of
convincing that yeah, you

00:33:51.273 --> 00:33:53.940
can ask for their parameters and
plug it into your little model.

00:33:53.940 --> 00:33:55.290
But all they have
to do is say, I

00:33:55.290 --> 00:33:56.832
don't know how this
model works, or I

00:33:56.832 --> 00:34:00.453
don't believe your model to not
want to drive their decisions.

00:34:00.453 --> 00:34:01.870
They're not going
to listen to it.

00:34:01.870 --> 00:34:05.440
So it was a very simplistic
model that they could--

00:34:05.440 --> 00:34:08.010
you took them along
each step and say, OK,

00:34:08.010 --> 00:34:10.139
if you've got this
many crews here,

00:34:10.139 --> 00:34:14.069
that means you could complete
how many SRs that day?

00:34:17.290 --> 00:34:21.449
The other half of that one was,
they used to divide the city up

00:34:21.449 --> 00:34:24.090
into quadrants, and they
had hard restrictions

00:34:24.090 --> 00:34:27.900
on how many crews they would
put in each quadrant, which

00:34:27.900 --> 00:34:31.920
meant if a quadrant-- if a
crew called off in a quadrant,

00:34:31.920 --> 00:34:34.530
it impacted that
quadrant but no one else.

00:34:34.530 --> 00:34:36.725
But the issue was,
if all the SRs,

00:34:36.725 --> 00:34:38.850
if all the demand was in
another part of the city--

00:34:38.850 --> 00:34:41.820
or in that part of the
city, you can pull a crew

00:34:41.820 --> 00:34:44.969
from elsewhere that had a
low workload into that area.

00:34:44.969 --> 00:34:48.330
So it was-- they were
placing arbitrary constraints

00:34:48.330 --> 00:34:50.010
on their deployments.

00:34:50.010 --> 00:34:53.580
We got them basically to
eliminate that way of working,

00:34:53.580 --> 00:34:56.400
that kind of strategy,
because we proved out

00:34:56.400 --> 00:35:02.760
that you're exacerbating the
issue by arbitrarily placing

00:35:02.760 --> 00:35:06.330
these constraints on your crews.

00:35:06.330 --> 00:35:08.250
So that was a really good win.

00:35:08.250 --> 00:35:10.110
And by the way, I
think there was a Sun

00:35:10.110 --> 00:35:12.450
article at the time about it.

00:35:12.450 --> 00:35:14.520
The Southeast
quadrant of the city

00:35:14.520 --> 00:35:17.910
was getting far better
service than the rest of the--

00:35:17.910 --> 00:35:18.790
rest of the city.

00:35:18.790 --> 00:35:21.670
So there was a
massive equity issue.

00:35:21.670 --> 00:35:23.522
So again, just by
some simple analysis,

00:35:23.522 --> 00:35:25.230
and showing them and
bringing them along,

00:35:25.230 --> 00:35:27.300
we were able to make--

00:35:27.300 --> 00:35:31.120
convince them to make
some better decisions.

00:35:31.120 --> 00:35:34.320
The most recent example of
getting folks to make decisions

00:35:34.320 --> 00:35:36.450
was--

00:35:36.450 --> 00:35:40.650
so DPW is one of those where
it's a little bit tougher to--

00:35:40.650 --> 00:35:42.150
they're a little
bit further behind,

00:35:42.150 --> 00:35:44.317
I would say, on their growth
curves in terms of data

00:35:44.317 --> 00:35:46.690
literacy and using
data to make decisions.

00:35:46.690 --> 00:35:47.440
At the other end--

00:35:47.440 --> 00:35:48.435
RAHA DASTGHEYB: They're
losing their leadership.

00:35:48.435 --> 00:35:49.920
JUSTIN ELSZASZ: What's that?

00:35:49.920 --> 00:35:51.545
They're also losing
their leadership.

00:35:51.545 --> 00:35:53.130
The head of DPW resigned.

00:35:53.130 --> 00:35:54.480
Yeah, just a couple of days--

00:35:54.480 --> 00:35:56.063
yeah, it was like
two days ago, right?

00:35:58.410 --> 00:36:01.360
But do they even collect the
right data in the first place?

00:36:01.360 --> 00:36:02.887
Is it available?

00:36:02.887 --> 00:36:05.220
Because I know you were talking
about the cleanup crews.

00:36:05.220 --> 00:36:10.245
I think they have 16 total,
which is nowhere near enough.

00:36:10.245 --> 00:36:14.170
And so there's something
that they need--

00:36:14.170 --> 00:36:16.920
I mean, you don't make
hiring decisions, obviously.

00:36:16.920 --> 00:36:19.680
There's some gap
of how they recruit

00:36:19.680 --> 00:36:22.470
and where they get people to
do what-- and DPW especially

00:36:22.470 --> 00:36:24.438
with solid waste.

00:36:24.438 --> 00:36:25.980
RAHA DASTGHEYB: But
across the board,

00:36:25.980 --> 00:36:28.050
are people collecting
the right type of data

00:36:28.050 --> 00:36:31.320
that allows you to do the
analysis, to make decisions?

00:36:31.320 --> 00:36:34.390
Or do you then request them to
make-- to collect that data?

00:36:34.390 --> 00:36:37.200
How do you do that?

00:36:37.200 --> 00:36:39.240
A lot of the--

00:36:39.240 --> 00:36:41.970
I mean, there's
diminishing returns, right?

00:36:41.970 --> 00:36:45.630
Like, so 311 definitely covers
a lot of our city services.

00:36:45.630 --> 00:36:50.280
And certainly for something
like public works,

00:36:50.280 --> 00:36:57.370
311 is meant to be the record
of the source of truth.

00:36:57.370 --> 00:37:00.210
And so yeah, we direct
residents to 311.

00:37:00.210 --> 00:37:02.340
That's what we
look at internally

00:37:02.340 --> 00:37:06.150
to determine service levels.

00:37:06.150 --> 00:37:10.470
And now that said,
we continue to have

00:37:10.470 --> 00:37:13.777
quality issues around 311,
like you raised, that--

00:37:13.777 --> 00:37:15.360
for example, there's
still the concern

00:37:15.360 --> 00:37:19.080
that something gets closed,
but the work wasn't performed.

00:37:19.080 --> 00:37:22.740
That issue-- like it's
completely dependent on what

00:37:22.740 --> 00:37:24.120
SR type you're looking at.

00:37:24.120 --> 00:37:27.750
Because there's 200 service
request types, and each of them

00:37:27.750 --> 00:37:30.000
is an entirely
different workflow,

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:32.415
it's a different crew, it's
different people involved.

00:37:35.350 --> 00:37:39.310
So anyways, all that to say,
I would say on the whole,

00:37:39.310 --> 00:37:42.210
we're collecting
the right data for--

00:37:42.210 --> 00:37:44.820
for some of the bigger areas.

00:37:44.820 --> 00:37:48.150
I think there are some smaller
areas where we definitely

00:37:48.150 --> 00:37:50.190
need better data.

00:37:50.190 --> 00:37:51.900
One that comes to mind is--

00:37:55.050 --> 00:38:00.210
it's related to illegal dumping,
but our transfer stations

00:38:00.210 --> 00:38:03.750
where a small hauler truck could
go and drop off their loads.

00:38:03.750 --> 00:38:07.860
Maybe they're cleaning up
debris from a construction

00:38:07.860 --> 00:38:10.050
site or something,
so all they do

00:38:10.050 --> 00:38:12.180
is they've got a
pickup truck and they

00:38:12.180 --> 00:38:14.400
run a truckload of debris.

00:38:14.400 --> 00:38:20.040
We have really long line waits
at those drop off stations.

00:38:20.040 --> 00:38:23.180
And we've always had the--

00:38:23.180 --> 00:38:25.277
we've always hypothesized
that because there

00:38:25.277 --> 00:38:27.110
are those long waits
and these guys are just

00:38:27.110 --> 00:38:31.010
trying to make money, that
if they see a long wait,

00:38:31.010 --> 00:38:34.070
they're going to leave and
go dump their load somewhere

00:38:34.070 --> 00:38:35.300
they shouldn't be dumping it.

00:38:35.300 --> 00:38:38.730
And so that exacerbates
illegal dumping issues.

00:38:38.730 --> 00:38:41.360
The problem is we
don't have either

00:38:41.360 --> 00:38:45.410
a digital licensing or check-in
process for the transfer

00:38:45.410 --> 00:38:46.350
stations.

00:38:46.350 --> 00:38:50.540
So we can't actually monitor
if you have a small hauler

00:38:50.540 --> 00:38:53.900
license, how often are
you actually checking

00:38:53.900 --> 00:38:56.720
into the transfer station
because if we see that you have

00:38:56.720 --> 00:38:59.360
an active small hauler license,
but you're never checking

00:38:59.360 --> 00:39:03.170
in to a transfer station, that
might be a signal that they're

00:39:03.170 --> 00:39:06.170
taking their loads elsewhere.

00:39:06.170 --> 00:39:08.840
But because we don't have any
kind of digital tools for doing

00:39:08.840 --> 00:39:12.050
that check-in process, we aren't
able to look at that issue

00:39:12.050 --> 00:39:13.140
all that closely.

00:39:13.140 --> 00:39:17.030
So there are definitely
pockets where we absolutely

00:39:17.030 --> 00:39:19.130
need better data.

00:39:19.130 --> 00:39:22.280
And again, it's always
a matter of cities

00:39:22.280 --> 00:39:23.720
are resource-strapped.

00:39:27.260 --> 00:39:30.960
And like you said, there's
a spectrum of leadership,

00:39:30.960 --> 00:39:32.510
in terms of being data-driven.

00:39:32.510 --> 00:39:39.350
So it's finding the
areas where you've

00:39:39.350 --> 00:39:47.830
got all of the right constraints
satisfied to do some good.

00:39:47.830 --> 00:39:50.980
Sorry, I feel like
that was a long answer.

00:39:50.980 --> 00:39:52.190
No, that's great.

00:39:52.190 --> 00:39:54.460
It's really interesting
to be able to dive

00:39:54.460 --> 00:39:56.830
deep into one of
these data problems

00:39:56.830 --> 00:39:59.390
and see all the
different sides of it.

00:39:59.390 --> 00:40:02.945
And I know there are quite
a few questions here.

00:40:02.945 --> 00:40:04.570
So I want to jump
over to a few others.

00:40:04.570 --> 00:40:08.350
But Eric, I see you had asked a
question in the chat a bit ago.

00:40:08.350 --> 00:40:09.650
Do you want to jump in?

00:40:09.650 --> 00:40:10.480
Sure.

00:40:10.480 --> 00:40:12.760
It's been fascinating to
hear your stories, Justin,

00:40:12.760 --> 00:40:15.610
and really exciting to
hear these perspectives.

00:40:15.610 --> 00:40:17.350
So I'm just kind of
curious, as somebody

00:40:17.350 --> 00:40:20.350
who really appreciates
the tool stack

00:40:20.350 --> 00:40:22.300
and how we're taking
modern approaches

00:40:22.300 --> 00:40:25.480
to data science, what
kind of modern tooling

00:40:25.480 --> 00:40:28.370
are you trying to implement
across your workflows,

00:40:28.370 --> 00:40:31.750
especially in the government
sector where, of course,

00:40:31.750 --> 00:40:34.990
my collaboration is mostly
with colleagues at the FDA.

00:40:34.990 --> 00:40:37.630
And I know they tend to have
some strict requirements

00:40:37.630 --> 00:40:39.537
in what they're allowed
to use and whatnot.

00:40:39.537 --> 00:40:41.120
So I'm just kind of
curious how you're

00:40:41.120 --> 00:40:44.050
handling those approaches
for best practice

00:40:44.050 --> 00:40:48.390
of code development or
releases within your team.

00:40:48.390 --> 00:40:53.910
Yeah, it's a joint effort
with our IT department

00:40:53.910 --> 00:41:01.470
because for the
ransomware attack we had--

00:41:01.470 --> 00:41:03.810
so we had a ransomware
attack here three years ago.

00:41:03.810 --> 00:41:06.000
And a lot of cities
have experienced these

00:41:06.000 --> 00:41:08.430
over the last couple of years.

00:41:08.430 --> 00:41:11.040
It really ground city
government completely to a halt.

00:41:11.040 --> 00:41:15.560
I couldn't access my email
for more than a month.

00:41:15.560 --> 00:41:19.520
Coming out of that, there's
been a pretty strong emphasis

00:41:19.520 --> 00:41:21.410
not only on cybersecurity,
but even just

00:41:21.410 --> 00:41:22.940
modernizing and
professionalizing

00:41:22.940 --> 00:41:23.945
our IT approach.

00:41:25.320 --> 00:41:28.610
And so our IT department has
grown by leaps and bounds

00:41:28.610 --> 00:41:29.255
in that regard.

00:41:34.710 --> 00:41:36.905
It's a joint effort
because this is

00:41:36.905 --> 00:41:38.280
one of those spaces
where there's

00:41:38.280 --> 00:41:41.370
a little bit of overlap between
IT and the technology side,

00:41:41.370 --> 00:41:47.460
but also data analytics
and that side of things

00:41:47.460 --> 00:41:51.090
and the data
governance around it.

00:41:51.090 --> 00:41:56.040
So things like-- we're just now
getting into what exactly is

00:41:56.040 --> 00:41:58.110
our code repo policy.

00:41:58.110 --> 00:42:01.530
Internally, we have Azure.

00:42:01.530 --> 00:42:07.320
The IT department is using
Azure DevOps now for code

00:42:07.320 --> 00:42:08.140
and deploying.

00:42:08.140 --> 00:42:12.570
And so getting our
data analysts onto that

00:42:12.570 --> 00:42:15.960
and getting them to recognize
the value in bringing that

00:42:15.960 --> 00:42:18.750
into Azure is important
because of course,

00:42:18.750 --> 00:42:21.210
on the data side and a lot
of the civic technology side,

00:42:21.210 --> 00:42:25.080
you also want to build
open source tools.

00:42:25.080 --> 00:42:30.143
And that's always my
preference as well.

00:42:30.143 --> 00:42:32.310
But a lot of folks who
either come into data science

00:42:32.310 --> 00:42:35.280
from other areas
aren't always up

00:42:35.280 --> 00:42:38.790
to speed on software
development best practices.

00:42:38.790 --> 00:42:41.700
And so you do get
folks writing code,

00:42:41.700 --> 00:42:45.120
maybe code that doesn't need to
be or doesn't belong on GitHub

00:42:45.120 --> 00:42:47.820
and really does actually
need to stay internally.

00:42:47.820 --> 00:42:51.450
So that's something we're
actually confronting right now,

00:42:51.450 --> 00:42:56.220
is let's get together a
policy where we all agree,

00:42:56.220 --> 00:42:58.950
this is a great use
case for open source.

00:42:58.950 --> 00:43:01.480
This is where you should
make something open source.

00:43:01.480 --> 00:43:03.870
And here are the
places where we can't

00:43:03.870 --> 00:43:07.930
make it open source and need
to keep things internal.

00:43:07.930 --> 00:43:09.930
So there are definitely
governance issues there.

00:43:09.930 --> 00:43:14.790
That's kind of a live
question for us Yeah,

00:43:14.790 --> 00:43:16.680
does that answer your question?

00:43:16.680 --> 00:43:18.930
I can't recall.

00:43:18.930 --> 00:43:20.980
No, that's quite
interesting to me.

00:43:20.980 --> 00:43:22.380
And I can sympathize.

00:43:22.380 --> 00:43:24.450
In fact, I was on a
meeting just before this

00:43:24.450 --> 00:43:27.840
where some of my colleagues
had never heard about GitHub

00:43:27.840 --> 00:43:29.100
in this other part of the org.

00:43:29.100 --> 00:43:31.570
And yeah, there's
lots of education

00:43:31.570 --> 00:43:33.570
that we even have to do
in our own organizations

00:43:33.570 --> 00:43:34.778
about some of these toolings.

00:43:34.778 --> 00:43:37.020
But yeah, certainly, it
sounds like you're thinking

00:43:37.020 --> 00:43:38.190
a lot of the great ideas.

00:43:38.190 --> 00:43:38.730
And I agree.

00:43:38.730 --> 00:43:40.290
There definitely
is a lot of overlap

00:43:40.290 --> 00:43:42.810
that can occur between
what you're proposing

00:43:42.810 --> 00:43:45.570
from a development
tooling perspective

00:43:45.570 --> 00:43:47.310
and what IT is
trying to serve up.

00:43:47.310 --> 00:43:50.150
So having that
relationship solid upfront

00:43:50.150 --> 00:43:53.190
is always very beneficial.

00:43:53.190 --> 00:43:56.520
And the other really
big aspect of it

00:43:56.520 --> 00:43:59.940
for city government or
any government, really,

00:43:59.940 --> 00:44:04.240
is that we're an organization
of organizations.

00:44:04.240 --> 00:44:06.120
So in some respects,
yes, we have

00:44:06.120 --> 00:44:11.790
central IT who can support
all of the other agencies.

00:44:11.790 --> 00:44:14.490
Up until the last couple
of years, a lot of agencies

00:44:14.490 --> 00:44:17.430
pursued their own
IT, whether they

00:44:17.430 --> 00:44:19.470
weren't getting good
support from central IT

00:44:19.470 --> 00:44:22.950
or not moving quickly enough or
wanted to try their own things

00:44:22.950 --> 00:44:24.450
or wanted to use
something different

00:44:24.450 --> 00:44:26.790
than everyone else was using.

00:44:26.790 --> 00:44:28.740
So there's been a
lot of-- so there's

00:44:28.740 --> 00:44:32.580
been a federated approach,
very distributed.

00:44:35.450 --> 00:44:39.840
And that applied to
the data folks as well.

00:44:39.840 --> 00:44:41.430
So there's an
argument to be made

00:44:41.430 --> 00:44:44.520
that there are things we
need to centralize and put

00:44:44.520 --> 00:44:46.170
more constraints around.

00:44:46.170 --> 00:44:47.820
The concern is
always, well, we don't

00:44:47.820 --> 00:44:49.110
want to slow down an agency.

00:44:49.110 --> 00:44:52.020
If they've got someone who knows
how to use data over there,

00:44:52.020 --> 00:44:53.890
we want them to--

00:44:53.890 --> 00:44:57.605
and the agency knows what
they want to do with it,

00:44:57.605 --> 00:44:58.980
we don't want to
be a bottleneck.

00:44:58.980 --> 00:45:00.480
And we don't want
to constrain them.

00:45:00.480 --> 00:45:04.110
We want to get them to use
that data how they need to.

00:45:04.110 --> 00:45:06.630
So there's a balancing act
there where we're like,

00:45:06.630 --> 00:45:08.850
yes, we need to be a
little bit more constrained

00:45:08.850 --> 00:45:11.830
and a little bit more
centralized with some things,

00:45:11.830 --> 00:45:16.050
but not at the
cost of innovation,

00:45:16.050 --> 00:45:20.220
agency responsiveness,
real world problem

00:45:20.220 --> 00:45:21.550
solving that needs to get done.

00:45:21.550 --> 00:45:25.860
So it is a challenge when
it's not-- in some respects,

00:45:25.860 --> 00:45:27.180
we're one organization.

00:45:27.180 --> 00:45:31.410
And in other respects,
we're 30 organizations.

00:45:31.410 --> 00:45:36.880
So that definitely
complicates it.

00:45:36.880 --> 00:45:40.840
Rory, I see you shared an
example of a citizen data

00:45:40.840 --> 00:45:43.210
science analysis in Baltimore.

00:45:43.210 --> 00:45:46.750
I would love to hear from you
and have you ask your question,

00:45:46.750 --> 00:45:47.680
too.

00:45:47.680 --> 00:45:49.000
Yeah, sure.

00:45:49.000 --> 00:45:50.350
Hey, Justin.

00:45:50.350 --> 00:45:50.860
I'm Rory.

00:45:50.860 --> 00:45:51.760
I live in Baltimore.

00:45:51.760 --> 00:45:53.260
I'm in Canton.

00:45:53.260 --> 00:45:56.200
A while ago, I don't know if you
guys have come across this yet,

00:45:56.200 --> 00:46:00.160
but on Reddit, there's a thread
of someone scraping the water

00:46:00.160 --> 00:46:04.640
bill page and taking a look
at who has outstanding bills.

00:46:04.640 --> 00:46:06.970
And in general, the
gist is that there

00:46:06.970 --> 00:46:09.310
is, I think it's
like $330 million

00:46:09.310 --> 00:46:12.490
in water bills that haven't been
paid-- a lot of them actually

00:46:12.490 --> 00:46:16.060
being often business
or corporate customers.

00:46:16.060 --> 00:46:19.210
I was curious because right
now, I work in people analytics.

00:46:19.210 --> 00:46:22.180
And I'm working on building
out a set of active and passive

00:46:22.180 --> 00:46:23.098
listening strategy.

00:46:23.098 --> 00:46:25.390
And so this is what's the
top of mind for me right now,

00:46:25.390 --> 00:46:33.160
is how are you guys collecting
the needs of the city

00:46:33.160 --> 00:46:36.010
and deciding what projects the
data team can get involved in

00:46:36.010 --> 00:46:38.860
and really help solve
some of those issues?

00:46:38.860 --> 00:46:40.230
So I would assume--

00:46:40.230 --> 00:46:41.980
I've talked to a lot
of people about this.

00:46:41.980 --> 00:46:43.605
They've seen this
Reddit thread before.

00:46:43.605 --> 00:46:46.240
And I'm sure it's made its way
through traditional channels

00:46:46.240 --> 00:46:48.850
to the government, as far as
working on the water bill.

00:46:48.850 --> 00:46:50.930
I'm sure you guys have
some exposure to it.

00:46:50.930 --> 00:46:52.810
But what I'm curious
about is if you guys

00:46:52.810 --> 00:46:54.760
are working on any kind
of listening strategy

00:46:54.760 --> 00:47:00.430
to pull in from outside of
traditional channels, ways

00:47:00.430 --> 00:47:03.070
of detecting problems that
are going on in the city.

00:47:03.070 --> 00:47:04.850
Yeah, that's a good question.

00:47:04.850 --> 00:47:11.000
I think every team, and I think
every office or department

00:47:11.000 --> 00:47:17.460
or agency or whatever, has
slightly different ways

00:47:17.460 --> 00:47:19.920
of doing that.

00:47:19.920 --> 00:47:25.740
And once we actually
have a central analytics

00:47:25.740 --> 00:47:28.560
team as opposed to CitiStat
or some of the other data

00:47:28.560 --> 00:47:31.095
functions, I think--

00:47:34.230 --> 00:47:37.350
some of the criteria that
of course come to mind

00:47:37.350 --> 00:47:40.530
are having a
project where you've

00:47:40.530 --> 00:47:43.440
got leadership buy-in, so that
if you dive into the problem

00:47:43.440 --> 00:47:45.482
and come up with something,
that they're actually

00:47:45.482 --> 00:47:49.530
going to be responsive to it
and know how to approach it.

00:47:50.580 --> 00:47:55.930
And as far as listening
goes, like I said,

00:47:55.930 --> 00:47:57.680
it depends on the area.

00:47:57.680 --> 00:48:00.170
But there are a few
different conduits.

00:48:00.170 --> 00:48:04.510
There's always going
to be that issue

00:48:04.510 --> 00:48:05.860
that the mayor cares about.

00:48:05.860 --> 00:48:07.818
There's always going to
be issues that the city

00:48:07.818 --> 00:48:09.220
council cares about.

00:48:09.220 --> 00:48:12.910
You have the Mayor's Office
of Constituent Services who

00:48:12.910 --> 00:48:16.480
deals directly with residents on
things that are maybe not just

00:48:16.480 --> 00:48:19.720
a straightforward 311 request,
but are maybe more lengthy

00:48:19.720 --> 00:48:20.785
or in-depth issues.

00:48:22.960 --> 00:48:27.220
Agencies all have their own
community liaisons and PIOs

00:48:27.220 --> 00:48:30.055
that are out in the community
and surfacing issues.

00:48:32.590 --> 00:48:36.670
For a data team,
yeah, I don't know

00:48:36.670 --> 00:48:39.250
how a data team
might choose based

00:48:39.250 --> 00:48:43.690
on all of these different inputs
and communication streams.

00:48:43.690 --> 00:48:47.260
Yeah, like I said, leadership
in data quality is another one.

00:48:47.260 --> 00:48:48.490
Is this an issue?

00:48:48.490 --> 00:48:51.220
Is the problem at
hand that we have

00:48:51.220 --> 00:48:53.980
data that might help
us address the problem,

00:48:53.980 --> 00:48:57.310
and we just need to put
some brainpower behind it?

00:48:57.310 --> 00:49:00.980
Or is it, well, when we
actually dig into it,

00:49:00.980 --> 00:49:02.800
we don't have data at all or.

00:49:02.800 --> 00:49:05.840
We don't have the right
data or good enough data.

00:49:05.840 --> 00:49:10.450
And so now, what we actually
need is to invest in a platform

00:49:10.450 --> 00:49:14.350
or create a new 311
service request type

00:49:14.350 --> 00:49:19.000
or put these people in contact
with constituent services.

00:49:19.000 --> 00:49:23.390
I don't think there's any
one cohesive strategy.

00:49:26.220 --> 00:49:28.800
Community engagement
is just messy.

00:49:28.800 --> 00:49:33.990
And by the way, I don't want
to speak for the administration

00:49:33.990 --> 00:49:37.470
because that's not
always my purview.

00:49:37.470 --> 00:49:39.975
But it's certainly
a huge aspect.

00:49:41.460 --> 00:49:44.160
And I try to regularly
do listening tours

00:49:44.160 --> 00:49:47.220
with the agencies
themselves-- so whether it's

00:49:47.220 --> 00:49:52.350
talking to agency heads or
deputy director of this bureau,

00:49:52.350 --> 00:49:55.830
or even the data analysts
themselves to surface issues.

00:50:00.330 --> 00:50:03.750
Yeah, there's no
shortage of issues

00:50:03.750 --> 00:50:08.310
to address or even
brainpower or good ideas.

00:50:08.310 --> 00:50:13.320
A lot of it is, there's just
limited ability to execute.

00:50:13.320 --> 00:50:16.530
So the real battle
is prioritizing

00:50:16.530 --> 00:50:20.230
and trying to keep focused
on something for long enough.

00:50:20.230 --> 00:50:20.730
Right.

00:50:20.730 --> 00:50:23.100
And discovering the
problems probably

00:50:23.100 --> 00:50:25.350
doesn't sound like it's a
very big issue for you guys.

00:50:25.350 --> 00:50:29.280
You have the channels to get at
what some of the problems are.

00:50:29.280 --> 00:50:33.180
Maybe a listing strategy that
is built out from the central

00:50:33.180 --> 00:50:36.330
would help you see who's
complaining about it the most.

00:50:36.330 --> 00:50:38.610
And that might help from a
prioritization perspective.

00:50:38.610 --> 00:50:41.790
But I mean, we're still
trying to figure that out

00:50:41.790 --> 00:50:43.500
even in private
companies who have

00:50:43.500 --> 00:50:45.510
a little bit more
control over how

00:50:45.510 --> 00:50:47.110
we spend our time sometimes.

00:50:47.110 --> 00:50:50.190
So I mean, I think it's
a relatively new thing

00:50:50.190 --> 00:50:52.780
that people are trying to talk
about and develop strategies

00:50:52.780 --> 00:50:53.280
around.

00:50:53.280 --> 00:50:55.230
I'm sure it'll take
you guys some time

00:50:55.230 --> 00:50:58.560
to get there once you've
determined it's a bigger

00:50:58.560 --> 00:51:00.385
need than it is right now.

00:51:00.385 --> 00:51:01.260
Yeah, and I think--

00:51:01.260 --> 00:51:02.677
Cool, thank you
for that answer.

00:51:02.677 --> 00:51:08.610
Yeah, I also think that
the 100,000-foot level,

00:51:08.610 --> 00:51:12.390
for Baltimore, the priority
areas are of course are public

00:51:12.390 --> 00:51:15.060
safety and city cleanliness.

00:51:15.060 --> 00:51:22.020
And so the issue is that
solutions to both of those

00:51:22.020 --> 00:51:28.040
can be completely based on
neighborhood within the city.

00:51:28.040 --> 00:51:30.860
It's very hyper-local,
both of those issues.

00:51:30.860 --> 00:51:37.340
And so for issues like that,
your qualitative information,

00:51:37.340 --> 00:51:40.790
the voice of the residents, is
just as important, if not more

00:51:40.790 --> 00:51:43.550
important, to make sure you've
got a sustainable solution

00:51:43.550 --> 00:51:45.650
to address those problems.

00:51:45.650 --> 00:51:48.020
The data that we have
centrally to maybe analyze

00:51:48.020 --> 00:51:50.780
some of these situations
may or may not

00:51:50.780 --> 00:51:54.310
tell us something at
a very local level.

00:51:54.310 --> 00:51:56.180
We may have [INAUDIBLE]
one data, yeah,

00:51:56.180 --> 00:51:59.240
we can zoom in on
a particular block.

00:51:59.240 --> 00:52:04.530
But for other issues, we may
not have data at that scale.

00:52:04.530 --> 00:52:08.123
And so you've got to go
listen to the residents.

00:52:08.123 --> 00:52:10.040
I have one quick
follow-up question, Rachel,

00:52:10.040 --> 00:52:11.510
