And resources are limited
pretty much everywhere,

00:54:13.930 --> 00:54:16.660
but specifically in
the public sector.

00:54:16.660 --> 00:54:19.820
And I'm in academic research.

00:54:19.820 --> 00:54:23.410
So I was wondering, how do you--

00:54:23.410 --> 00:54:25.120
what are some key
lessons as to how

00:54:25.120 --> 00:54:32.140
to navigate that sort of change
leadership part of things?

00:54:32.140 --> 00:54:36.540
Yeah, oh my god,
how long do we have?

00:54:36.540 --> 00:54:38.090
You have three--

00:54:38.090 --> 00:54:39.640
you have 300 words.

00:54:42.550 --> 00:54:48.740
Oh man, yeah, one
thing I've learned,

00:54:48.740 --> 00:54:52.460
and I think this was probably
a mistake of mine early

00:54:52.460 --> 00:54:59.290
on in the CDO
role, was there's--

00:54:59.290 --> 00:55:02.530
so when it comes
to data governance,

00:55:02.530 --> 00:55:07.530
there's a variety of different
issues you could be tackling.

00:55:07.530 --> 00:55:10.690
There's different approaches.

00:55:10.690 --> 00:55:15.880
What I had convinced
myself of was the need for,

00:55:15.880 --> 00:55:18.670
let's stand up this broad
data governance committee

00:55:18.670 --> 00:55:19.510
for the city.

00:55:19.510 --> 00:55:21.850
Every agency will
have a representative.

00:55:21.850 --> 00:55:25.570
We're going to take one
issue area at a time.

00:55:25.570 --> 00:55:29.290
But everyone, every agency,
every representative

00:55:29.290 --> 00:55:31.280
needs to make some
progress on that area.

00:55:31.280 --> 00:55:34.930
So if it's data quality, we
created this data quality

00:55:34.930 --> 00:55:38.560
guidebook or toolkit.

00:55:38.560 --> 00:55:44.530
And so step by step,
the idea was that month

00:55:44.530 --> 00:55:46.960
after month, every
day [INAUDIBLE],,

00:55:46.960 --> 00:55:49.570
but make some kind of progress.

00:55:49.570 --> 00:55:50.980
Sorry, did I freeze?

00:55:50.980 --> 00:55:52.025
Can you guys hear me?

00:55:52.025 --> 00:55:52.900
Yeah, a little bit.

00:55:52.900 --> 00:55:53.710
But [INAUDIBLE].

00:55:53.710 --> 00:55:54.970
I thought it was on my end.

00:55:54.970 --> 00:55:58.520
I was just checking my Wi-Fi.

00:55:58.520 --> 00:56:06.130
OK, I think that was probably
the wrong approach, especially

00:56:06.130 --> 00:56:09.280
in an environment of
such limited bandwidth,

00:56:09.280 --> 00:56:13.090
not only from the agency side,
but from my own bandwidth.

00:56:13.090 --> 00:56:18.790
I think I'm having a lot
more success in doubling down

00:56:18.790 --> 00:56:21.820
on the areas where
there is obvious buy-in,

00:56:21.820 --> 00:56:27.610
and people want to
change something.

00:56:27.610 --> 00:56:32.620
Trying to get everyone to move
an inch in the same direction

00:56:32.620 --> 00:56:34.180
was a real grind.

00:56:34.180 --> 00:56:38.170
And I'm not convinced it has
the same impact as finding

00:56:38.170 --> 00:56:40.990
the one or two people who
want to go a couple of miles

00:56:40.990 --> 00:56:41.650
with you.

00:56:43.210 --> 00:56:49.710
So I think that's been one
of my big lessons learned

00:56:49.710 --> 00:56:51.210
over the last two years.

00:56:51.210 --> 00:56:53.130
And maybe that's
only when you're

00:56:53.130 --> 00:56:58.320
so resource-constrained
as to be an office of one.

00:56:58.320 --> 00:57:04.320
But yeah, I think that's one
of my main lessons learned

00:57:04.320 --> 00:57:07.140
on that front.

00:57:07.140 --> 00:57:09.510
Of course, all of the other
typical change management

00:57:09.510 --> 00:57:14.340
advice applies, right, like
listening and making sure

00:57:14.340 --> 00:57:17.760
you're actually solving someone
else's problem and not just

00:57:17.760 --> 00:57:22.290
your problem or what you
perceive as being the problem.

00:57:22.290 --> 00:57:25.020
But yeah, I don't know.

00:57:25.020 --> 00:57:26.110
Does that help?

00:57:26.110 --> 00:57:27.480
Yeah, definitely.

00:57:27.480 --> 00:57:29.980
I definitely want to leave
time for more questions.

00:57:29.980 --> 00:57:32.560
But I think I'm seeing
some repeating themes.

00:57:32.560 --> 00:57:35.520
One is like, it seems like
feudalism is still alive

00:57:35.520 --> 00:57:39.210
and well, even in
the 21st century.

00:57:39.210 --> 00:57:40.710
And that's something
to navigate.

00:57:40.710 --> 00:57:43.150
But also the point
that you raise,

00:57:43.150 --> 00:57:48.180
which is if you don't
necessarily have the authority

00:57:48.180 --> 00:57:53.260
to just tell people what to do,
especially in other agencies,

00:57:53.260 --> 00:57:57.842
then I think it's good to
find people who are already

00:57:57.842 --> 00:57:58.800
willing to go with you.

00:57:58.800 --> 00:58:00.660
And then build your
success stories.

00:58:00.660 --> 00:58:03.210
And then say, hey, we did this.

00:58:03.210 --> 00:58:06.240
And this indicator
went up for whatever

00:58:06.240 --> 00:58:08.250
the good direction was.

00:58:08.250 --> 00:58:10.590
We saw a massive
improvement with this person

00:58:10.590 --> 00:58:13.530
who was willing to
go the extra mile

00:58:13.530 --> 00:58:15.840
or do a little bit
of a side project

00:58:15.840 --> 00:58:18.500
and then build that as a success
story for others, I guess.

00:58:18.500 --> 00:58:21.630
Yeah, even when you
are the person that

00:58:21.630 --> 00:58:27.030
has the authority to say,
yeah, you have to do this--

00:58:27.030 --> 00:58:31.950
look, I report to the
city administrator,

00:58:31.950 --> 00:58:35.970
who, like I said, is kind of
the chief operating officer.

00:58:35.970 --> 00:58:39.840
They early on were more
involved in setting

00:58:39.840 --> 00:58:42.330
some of the expectations
around data governance.

00:58:42.330 --> 00:58:49.150
But agencies are under
countless, countless unfunded

00:58:49.150 --> 00:58:52.515
mandates that, the mayor
wants him to do this.

00:58:52.515 --> 00:58:53.890
These are the
things that they're

00:58:53.890 --> 00:58:55.390
legally obligated to do.

00:58:55.390 --> 00:58:57.100
They've got citizens
wanting them

00:58:57.100 --> 00:59:01.600
to go in this
direction, all of whom

00:59:01.600 --> 00:59:04.180
have a right to
claim that they have

00:59:04.180 --> 00:59:06.820
a voice in what that
agency is doing.

00:59:06.820 --> 00:59:09.170
And I'm just one
of those voices.

00:59:09.170 --> 00:59:13.690
So yeah, I report to
the city administrator.

00:59:13.690 --> 00:59:14.888
I'm the chief data officer.

00:59:14.888 --> 00:59:16.180
That comes with some authority.

00:59:16.180 --> 00:59:21.730
But that still doesn't mean
the thing is going to get done.

00:59:21.730 --> 00:59:24.460
And not only that.

00:59:24.460 --> 00:59:28.720
If I take the approach of,
well, I'm in the mayor's office,

00:59:28.720 --> 00:59:31.660
so you have to listen to
me, it's going to backfire.

00:59:31.660 --> 00:59:34.510
And people aren't going
to want to work with me.

00:59:34.510 --> 00:59:39.610
So that card is best kept
for as long as possible

00:59:39.610 --> 00:59:44.560
in your back pocket because it
just burns bridges so quickly.

00:59:44.560 --> 00:59:49.600
So yeah, the idea
is really, no matter

00:59:49.600 --> 00:59:53.830
what, to find a way to ensure
folks that what they're doing

00:59:53.830 --> 00:59:57.100
is in their best
interest genuinely,

00:59:57.100 --> 01:00:01.300
not just selling
them on something.

01:00:01.300 --> 01:00:04.390
Yeah, yeah, it's
definitely tough.

01:00:04.390 --> 01:00:05.540
But yeah, I appreciate it.

01:00:05.540 --> 01:00:06.100
Thank you.

01:00:06.100 --> 01:00:06.600
Yeah.

01:00:08.910 --> 01:00:10.470
Thank you so much, Justin.

01:00:10.470 --> 01:00:12.193
And I know we're at
the top of the hour.

01:00:12.193 --> 01:00:14.860
And a bunch of people might have
to drop to other meetings, too.

01:00:14.860 --> 01:00:17.100
But I did want to just
ask one more question.

01:00:17.100 --> 01:00:18.840
And I can't remember
who asked it.

01:00:18.840 --> 01:00:24.960
It was maybe Sepp or Alan,
was asking about cross-city

01:00:24.960 --> 01:00:31.140
collaboration between different
data teams, and if there's--

01:00:31.140 --> 01:00:33.970
I'm not remembering
exactly the question,

01:00:33.970 --> 01:00:37.320
but if there's a way to connect
with other city governments

01:00:37.320 --> 01:00:38.440
and work together?

01:00:38.440 --> 01:00:43.320
And I think I've seen this a lot
in the pharma space recently,

01:00:43.320 --> 01:00:46.020
in all these big pharma
companies working together

01:00:46.020 --> 01:00:47.580
and creating packages--

01:00:47.580 --> 01:00:52.230
curious if there's something
that exists in government.

01:00:52.230 --> 01:00:55.770
Yes, I often tell folks
that's one of the best

01:00:55.770 --> 01:00:59.690
parts of working
in government, is

01:00:59.690 --> 01:01:03.350
we don't have to worry
about protected information

01:01:03.350 --> 01:01:06.470
or competition.

01:01:06.470 --> 01:01:09.815
And so there are a few
different networks.

01:01:12.450 --> 01:01:17.130
A couple that we purchased--
so Bloomberg Philanthropies is

01:01:17.130 --> 01:01:19.630
pretty big in the
local government space.

01:01:19.630 --> 01:01:21.930
So Michael Bloomberg's
theory of change

01:01:21.930 --> 01:01:24.870
was that, if you really
want to make an impact

01:01:24.870 --> 01:01:28.260
and impact people's lives,
local government is the place

01:01:28.260 --> 01:01:32.550
to be because at the
federal level, yeah,

01:01:32.550 --> 01:01:34.800
you've got very
wide, broad impact.

01:01:34.800 --> 01:01:38.490
But it is very hard to
see this trillion dollar

01:01:38.490 --> 01:01:43.020
budget trickle down to see how
it impacts someone's lives.

01:01:43.020 --> 01:01:46.290
Local government is where
that fund money eventually

01:01:46.290 --> 01:01:49.140
comes into your
programs and projects.

01:01:49.140 --> 01:01:53.668
And so the Bloomberg
network, there

01:01:53.668 --> 01:01:55.710
are a couple of different
networks that they run.

01:01:55.710 --> 01:01:57.180
There's the innovation teams.

01:01:57.180 --> 01:02:00.960
They all, I mean, within the
first couple of months of me

01:02:00.960 --> 01:02:04.650
joining the I team, we
were visiting Los Angeles

01:02:04.650 --> 01:02:08.700
to meet with the other I
teams, and specifically

01:02:08.700 --> 01:02:11.490
with Los Angeles's I team
because they were working

01:02:11.490 --> 01:02:15.970
on the same issue as we
were going to be working on,

01:02:15.970 --> 01:02:19.850
which was the police
recruiting and hiring.

01:02:19.850 --> 01:02:23.510
They also run something
called the City Data

01:02:23.510 --> 01:02:26.150
Alliance, which is
a cohort right now

01:02:26.150 --> 01:02:28.100
of about 20 or 25 cities.

01:02:28.100 --> 01:02:32.120
We get technical assistance
around a particular track.

01:02:32.120 --> 01:02:35.460
There's executive leadership
training for using data.

01:02:35.460 --> 01:02:37.280
So that's another network.

01:02:37.280 --> 01:02:41.360
I'm part of, it's called
the Civic Analytics Network,

01:02:41.360 --> 01:02:44.600
which is a network of chief
data officers from cities

01:02:44.600 --> 01:02:46.430
around the country.

01:02:46.430 --> 01:02:50.690
That's run out of
Harvard Kennedy School

01:02:50.690 --> 01:02:53.990
by Professor Stephen Goldsmith.

01:02:53.990 --> 01:02:55.430
He was a mayor of Indianapolis.

01:02:55.430 --> 01:02:58.410
He was a deputy
mayor of New York.

01:02:58.410 --> 01:03:01.520
So yeah, like I
said, there's tons.

01:03:01.520 --> 01:03:05.780
And through that network, I'll
call up the chief data officer

01:03:05.780 --> 01:03:08.060
from Pittsburgh
or Boston and ask

01:03:08.060 --> 01:03:11.570
how they're tackling this
problem or what piece of data

01:03:11.570 --> 01:03:13.830
stack they're using to
solve different things.

01:03:13.830 --> 01:03:17.630
So that's one of the best
parts of the city government

01:03:17.630 --> 01:03:21.000
is just being able
to cold call people

01:03:21.000 --> 01:03:23.430
from other sides of the
country and figure out

01:03:23.430 --> 01:03:24.690
how they solve something.

01:03:27.303 --> 01:03:28.470
Thank you so much, Justin.

01:03:28.470 --> 01:03:30.810
I tried to capture some of
those links and share them.

01:03:30.810 --> 01:03:33.270
But maybe after, I
can email you for some

01:03:33.270 --> 01:03:34.740
to share with the
recording, too.

01:03:34.740 --> 01:03:35.682
Sure.

01:03:35.682 --> 01:03:37.140
Because I saw a
lot of people who

01:03:37.140 --> 01:03:40.500
are sharing different open
data portals in their cities,

01:03:40.500 --> 01:03:44.400
too, which I can collect
those all together as well.

01:03:44.400 --> 01:03:45.690
Yeah.

01:03:45.690 --> 01:03:47.730
I know you have a
2 and 1/2 year old

01:03:47.730 --> 01:03:50.550
who's trying to take
a nap pretty soon, so.

01:03:50.550 --> 01:03:51.660
No, that's the problem.

01:03:51.660 --> 01:03:53.130
He's not trying to take a nap.

01:03:53.130 --> 01:03:54.000
That's the problem.

01:03:54.000 --> 01:03:56.460
He's trying not to.

01:03:56.460 --> 01:03:59.340
I might like to go take
a nap right now, but.

01:03:59.340 --> 01:04:01.140
Yeah, same.

01:04:01.140 --> 01:04:03.810
I just wanted to
check if anybody

01:04:03.810 --> 01:04:06.420
is on that had a question you
wanted to ask so we didn't get

01:04:06.420 --> 01:04:08.615
to you, I'll give
another opportunity

01:04:08.615 --> 01:04:09.615
to raise your hand here.

01:04:12.830 --> 01:04:16.270
I think a few people that
asked earlier had to drop, but.

01:04:18.940 --> 01:04:20.950
Let's see-- double check.

01:04:20.950 --> 01:04:22.150
One hand, there it is.

01:04:22.150 --> 01:04:24.790
Hi, Barry.

01:04:24.790 --> 01:04:26.260
Hi, everyone.

01:04:26.260 --> 01:04:27.990
Mine is not a question.

01:04:27.990 --> 01:04:30.370
This is my first
time of joining.

01:04:30.370 --> 01:04:31.770
Thanks for joining.

01:04:31.770 --> 01:04:36.780
Yes, I'm joining from
